      "Read the Bible and pray together": 1,
      "With a parent or caregiver, read Psalm 92:13. Talk about what it means to flourish like a palm tree or a cedar of Lebanon.": 1,
      "The final page offers a question, a Bible reading, a small action and a short prayer. A trusted adult can read it aloud and stop to talk about any part.": 1,
      "About this story": 1,
      "Illustrations are imaginative composites, not portraits or eyewitness scenes. Maroun's life is known mostly from one chapter by Theodoret of Cyrrhus, who never met him, so the story leaves out his childhood and family because nobody knows them. The letter from John Chrysostom is addressed to a monk named Maron, and many people believe he meant this Maroun. The Maronite Church traces its name to him, though its early history is still debated by historians.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Who was Saint Maroun?": 1,
      "A hermit of the late 300s who lived on a hilltop near Cyrrhus in northern Syria. The Maronite Church is named after him. His feast is on 9 February.": 1,
      "Is the story true?": 1,
      "It follows the bishop Theodoret's chapter about Maroun, the one close account of his life. Where the sources are silent, the story says so and invents nothing.": 1,
      "Who is this storybook for?": 1,
      "Children ages 6 to 12, reading or listening with family. Ten illustrated pages end with a four-part family reflection.": 1,
